Learning objectives
By the end of this session, students should be able to:
1. Understand how Git maintains project history.
2. View and inspect previous commits.
3. Compare different versions of files.
4. Identify changes before committing.
5. Undo modifications safely.
6. Delete files from Git tracking.
7. Understand Git Reset and Git Revert.
8. Recover previous commits using Git Reflog.
9. Understand the difference between Soft, Mixed and Hard Reset.

PART 1 – Understanding Git Commit History
1. What is Git history?
First, explain a real-life scenario.

Suppose a developer creates a bakery website.

On Monday, the developer creates the homepage.

On Tuesday, the developer adds a navigation menu.

On Wednesday, the developer adds a login page.

On Thursday, the developer accidentally modifies the homepage and breaks the design.

Now the developer wants to return to Wednesday's version.

How can this be done?

This is where Git history becomes useful.

Git maintains a record of committed changes, allowing developers to inspect earlier versions and recover previous project states.
Visual explanation

Commit 1          Commit 2          Commit 3          Commit 4
   |                 |                 |                 |
   v                 v                 v                 v

Initial         Added Menu        Added Login       Updated CSS
Website         Navigation        Functionality     Design

------------------------------------------------------------>
                     Project Timeline


 Every commit represents a snapshot of the project.
Note: "Git is not just a tool for saving code. It is a time machine for your project."           

PART 2 – Understanding Git Log
2. What is git log?
The git log command displays the commit history of a repository.

Let's perform a practical.
Practical 1: Create a project with multiple commits
Step 1. Create a directory.

mkdir git-history-lab
cd git-history-lab

Step 2. Initialize Git.
git init

Step 3. Configure your identity if not already configured.
git config --global user.name "Student"
git config --global user.email "student@example.com"

Step 4. Create a file.
nano index.html

Add:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
<p>Fresh cakes and bakery products.</p>

Step 5. Stage and commit.
git add index.html
git commit -m "Initial bakery website"

Step 6. Modify the file.
nano index.html

Add another line:
<p>We provide fresh cakes, cookies and pastries.</p>

Commit again:
git add index.html
git commit -m "Added bakery product description"

Step 7. Modify the file again.
<h2>Our Special Cakes</h2>

Commit:
git add index.html
git commit -m "Added special cakes section"

Now execute:
git log

3. Useful variations of Git Log
A. One-line history
git log --oneline

Output:
8f43c21 Added special cakes section
6a12b45 Added bakery product description
3b29c87 Initial bakery website

This is useful when there are hundreds of commits.
B. Graphical history
git log --graph --oneline

This displays commit history as a graph.
C. Show all branches
git log --all --oneline --graph --decorate

This becomes particularly useful when students start working with branches.
D. Display the last three commits
git log -3

E. Display commits by author
git log --author="Student"

F. Display commits with date information
git log --pretty=fuller

If a developer wants to know who committed a particular change and when, which command should they use?

Answer: git log.

PART 3 – Understanding Git Show
4. What is git show?
git show displays detailed information about a particular Git object, commonly a commit.

For example:
git show

This displays information about the latest commit.

You can also inspect a particular commit:
git show 8f43c21

Here, 8f43c21 is an example commit ID. Students should use their own commit ID.
What does Git Show display?
- Commit ID.
- Author.
- Date.
- Commit message.
- Files changed.
- Actual content differences.
Practical demonstration
First:
git log --oneline

Copy the commit ID.

Then:
git show <commit-id>

Explain to students:

"git log tells us which commits exist. git show helps us understand what happened inside a particular commit."
PART 4 – Understanding Git Diff
5. What is Git Diff?
This is one of the most frequently used commands in professional development.

Suppose your committed HTML file contains:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
<p>Fresh cakes and bakery products.</p>

Now modify it:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake Bakery</h1>
<p>Fresh cakes, cookies and pastries.</p>

Execute:
git diff

You may see:
-<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>
+<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake Bakery</h1>

-<p>Fresh cakes and bakery products.</p>
+<p>Fresh cakes, cookies and pastries.</p>

Explain the output
- - means removed content.
- + means added content.
Important Git Diff variations
Command	Explanation
git diff	Working directory vs staging area
git diff --staged	Staging area vs latest commit
git diff HEAD	Combined working-directory and staged changes vs HEAD
git diff HEAD~1 HEAD	Compare previous commit with latest commit
git diff <commit1> <commit2>	Compare two commits


Practical: Compare two commits
git log --oneline

Copy two commit IDs.

Run:
git diff <commit1> <commit2>

This shows how the project changed between those two versions.

Important teaching point: Git Diff helps developers inspect changes before committing them. It is particularly useful in code reviews.
PART 5 – Undoing Changes in Git
Now comes the most important practical section.

Tell students:

"Developers make mistakes. Sometimes they modify the wrong file, stage unwanted changes or commit incorrect code. Git provides different mechanisms to recover from these situations."

There are several commands, and each solves a different problem.
6. Understanding Git Restore
git restore is used to restore file contents.

It can discard unstaged modifications or restore files from a specified source.
Scenario 1: Accidentally modified a file
Suppose the original file is:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>

You accidentally change it to:
<h1>Welcome to The Worst Bakery</h1>

Check:
git status

Git reports:
modified: index.html

Now you want to discard the modification.

Execute:
git restore index.html

Check the file.
cat index.html

The file returns to its content in the index (normally the latest commit if nothing was staged).

Explain: Git Restore discards the unstaged changes in the working directory.
Scenario 2: Unstage a file
Suppose:
git add index.html

You accidentally staged the wrong file.

Now execute:
git restore --staged index.html

The file is removed from the staging area, but its modifications remain in the working directory.

Check:
git status

You should now see the file as modified rather than staged.
Important distinction
Command	Result
git restore index.html	Discards unstaged modifications
git restore --staged index.html	Removes changes from staging but preserves working-directory changes


Warning: git restore can permanently discard uncommitted work. Teach students to inspect git diff before using it.
PART 6 – Git RM and Git MV
7. What is Git RM?
Suppose we want to remove contact.html from our bakery project.

We can execute:
git rm contact.html

This removes the file from the working directory and stages its deletion.

Then:
git commit -m "Removed contact page"

What if we want to stop tracking a file but keep it locally?
Execute:
git rm --cached filename

Example:
git rm --cached config.txt

This removes the file from Git's tracking index while keeping the working-directory copy.

This is useful when a file should remain on the developer's computer but should no longer be tracked by Git.

Important: If a file contains passwords or API keys, removing it from the current commit does not erase the secret from previous commits. The secret should be rotated immediately.
8. What is Git MV?
git mv is used to rename or move a tracked file.

Example:
git mv about.html about-us.html

This renames the file and stages the corresponding changes.

Commit:
git commit -m "Renamed about page"

You can also move a file into a directory:
mkdir pages
git mv about-us.html pages/

PART 7 – Git Reset: Soft vs Mixed vs Hard
This is a very important topic. Spend sufficient time here.
9. What is Git Reset?
Git Reset moves the current branch reference (HEAD) to another commit.

Depending on the reset mode, it also changes the staging area and working directory.

Imagine this commit history:

A ---- B ---- C ---- D
                  HEAD

 Suppose we want to move back from commit D to commit C.

Command:
git reset <commit-C>

But what happens to the changes from commit D?

That depends on the reset mode.
A. Git Reset --Soft
git reset --soft HEAD~1

Meaning:
- Moves HEAD backwards by one commit.
- Keeps changes from the removed commit staged.
- Does not discard the changes from the working directory.
Example:
Before:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D

After:
A ---- B ---- C
             HEAD

Changes from D remain staged.

Use case: You committed something but want to undo the commit while keeping the changes ready to recommit.
B. Git Reset --Mixed
git reset --mixed HEAD~1

Meaning:
- Moves HEAD backwards.
- Resets the staging area.
- Keeps the file changes in the working directory.
Example:
Before:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D

After:
A ---- B ---- C
             HEAD

Changes from D remain unstaged.

This is the default mode of git reset.

Therefore:
git reset HEAD~1

is equivalent to:
git reset --mixed HEAD~1

Use case: You want to undo the last commit but retain your changes so you can modify or selectively stage them again.
C. Git Reset --Hard
git reset --hard HEAD~1

Meaning:
- Moves HEAD backwards.
- Resets the staging area.
- Resets tracked working-directory files to match the target commit.
Example:
Before:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D

After:
A ---- B ---- C
             HEAD

Changes from D are discarded.

Warning: This can destroy uncommitted changes and make the removed commit unreachable through the branch. Recovery may be possible using reflog, but students must never treat that as guaranteed.
Comparison table
Feature	Soft	Mixed	Hard
Moves HEAD	Yes	Yes	Yes
Resets staging area	No	Yes	Yes
Resets tracked working files	No	No	Yes
Keeps changes staged	Yes	No	No
Keeps changes unstaged	No	Yes	No


Classroom demonstration
Create three commits:
echo "Version 1" > demo.txt
git add demo.txt
git commit -m "Version 1"

echo "Version 2" >> demo.txt
git add demo.txt
git commit -m "Version 2"

echo "Version 3" >> demo.txt
git add demo.txt
git commit -m "Version 3"

Check:
git log --oneline

Now execute:
git reset --soft HEAD~1

Check:
git status
git log --oneline

Observe that Version 3's changes remain staged.

For the next demonstrations, restore the original history or create a fresh repository so each reset mode can be demonstrated independently.

Repeat with:
git reset --mixed HEAD~1

And finally, on a disposable test repository:
git reset --hard HEAD~1

Ask students to observe the difference after each command.
PART 8 – Git Revert: Safely Undo a Commit
10. What is Git Revert?
This is particularly important in professional environments.

Suppose five developers are working on a shared project.

A developer makes a commit that introduces a bug.

The commit has already been pushed to GitHub.

Should we use git reset --hard?

Usually, no.

Why?

Because resetting a shared branch rewrites its history and can cause problems for other developers.

Instead, we generally use:
git revert <commit-id>

Git creates a new commit that reverses the changes introduced by the selected commit.
Visual explanation
Before:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D
                  HEAD

Suppose D introduced a bug.

After:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D ---- E
                              HEAD

E = Reverses changes introduced by D

Notice something important:

The original commit D still exists in the history.

Git creates a new commit E to reverse its changes.
Practical
First check history:
git log --oneline

Copy the commit ID that introduced the unwanted change.

Execute:
git revert <commit-id>

Git may open an editor for the revert commit message.

You can also use:
git revert --no-edit <commit-id>

Teaching point:
- reset moves the branch reference and can remove commits from the branch history.
- revert preserves existing history and creates a new commit that reverses an earlier commit.
For shared branches, revert is generally the safer choice.
PART 9 – Git Commit Amend
11. What is Git Commit Amend?
Imagine you have committed a file:
git add index.html
git commit -m "Added home page"

Immediately afterward, you realize that you forgot to include style.css.

Instead of creating another commit, you can amend the previous commit.

Execute:
git add style.css
git commit --amend

This updates the previous commit.

If you only want to correct the commit message:
git commit --amend -m "Added homepage and styling"

Important: Amend replaces the previous commit with a new commit. Avoid amending commits that have already been shared unless you understand the implications of rewriting history.
PART 10 – Git Reflog: Recovering Lost Commits
This is an excellent advanced demonstration for students.
12. What is Git Reflog?
Git Reflog records updates to local references such as HEAD and branch tips.

It helps developers locate previous positions of HEAD, including after reset operations.

Suppose:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D

You accidentally execute:
git reset --hard HEAD~1

Now your branch points to C instead of D.

You think your last commit is lost.

This is where Reflog helps.

Execute:
git reflog

Example output:
abc1234 HEAD@{0}: reset: moving to HEAD~1
def5678 HEAD@{1}: commit: Added payment page
ghi9012 HEAD@{2}: commit: Added login page

Here, def5678 may be the commit you want to recover.

To recover it:
git reset --hard def5678

Warning: This command moves the current branch back to that commit and resets tracked files. Use it only after checking the current state and ensuring valuable work is protected.

An even safer first step is to create a recovery branch:
git branch recovery def5678

Now the commit is reachable through the recovery branch.
Important distinction
Command	Purpose
git log	Displays reachable commit history
git reflog	Displays local reference movement history


Tell students:

"Git Log tells you what commits are reachable in your current history. Git Reflog helps you find where your HEAD and branches were previously pointing."
PART 11 – Complete Practical: Recovering a Broken Bakery Website
Now combine everything.
Scenario
You are the developer of The Fairy Bake website.

Your tasks:
1. Create the project.
2. Make three commits.
3. Inspect commit history.
4. Modify a file.
5. Compare changes.
6. Restore an accidental modification.
7. Create an incorrect commit.
8. Revert the incorrect commit.
9. Reset a commit using Soft mode.
10. Recover a previous commit using Reflog.
Step 1. Create project
mkdir fairy-bake-history
cd fairy-bake-history
git init

Step 2. Create first version
echo "<h1>The Fairy Bake</h1>" > index.html

git add .
git commit -m "Initial bakery website"

Step 3. Add product section
echo "<h2>Our Products</h2>" >> index.html

git add .
git commit -m "Added products section"

Step 4. Add contact section
echo "<h2>Contact Us</h2>" >> index.html

git add .
git commit -m "Added contact section"

Step 5. Inspect history
git log --oneline

Step 6. Modify the file incorrectly
echo "<h1>Broken Website</h1>" >> index.html

Inspect:
git diff

Discard the modification:
git restore index.html

Step 7. Create an unwanted commit
echo "<h2>Temporary Section</h2>" >> index.html

git add .
git commit -m "Added temporary section"

Step 8. Revert the commit
git revert HEAD

Now check:
git log --oneline

Observe that Git has created a new commit reversing the temporary section.
Step 9. Understand Reflog
git reflog

Discuss the reference movements and recovery options.                 
